+# Batch 68 authoring notes — coupled Penrose vanishing
+
+## Scope and controlling inputs
+
+Own only the pair `qftx-coupled-penrose-vanishing-on-the-round-four-sphere` and its examples page. No assigned design section was found in the dated prose scaffold or its 143-item proposed inventory; the exact owner-approved appended declaration is in `owner-resolution/append2-new-batch-68.pages.proposed.json`. Its full statement and five proof obligations are retained. The scope and statement are controlled by the current `research/plan-spec.json` row and the newer run owner direction.
+
+The current plan and live manifest require five earlier pages, including `divisors-riemann-roch-and-duality`; the older dispatch brief and the pre-append proposal list only four. Preserve the current five-page list and add `qftx-gaga-projective-scalar-dolbeault` as required by the post-append owner handoff. The actual batch-68 manifest had no item inventory and no analytic-scalar edge; both are authoring obligations.
+
+The theorem keeps the approved positive stereographic orientation, incidence formula, all smooth finite-rank unitary ASD bundles, both `F` and `F^vee`, reducible and flat summands, and the no-GAGA/no-monad/no-inverse-ADHM scope. The source uses a self-dual convention; the assigned incidence is checked directly against `dx0 ^ dx1 ^ dx2 ^ dx3`, where the incidence alpha planes annihilate precisely the positive-orientation ASD curvature components. The unit sphere has scalar curvature `R=12`, so the correct zeroth-order coefficient is `R/6=2`.
+
+## Dependency and readiness audit
+
+Read the actual statements and proofs of `def-axiom-of-choice`, `def-complex-manifolds-and-holomorphic-vector-bundles`, `thm-first-hypercohomology-spectral-sequence`, `thm-cohomology-projective-space-twisting-sheaves`, the compact Hodge-representative theorem, and the compact-Riemann-surface harmonic-star/Serre-duality suppliers. The scheme-theoretic twisting-sheaf theorem is algebraic cohomology on `Proj`; it does not compare holomorphic sheaf cohomology with Dolbeault cohomology. The current proof therefore does not use that theorem as an analytic premise; the page remains in `requires` as current-plan context. The original planned `thm-first-hypercohomology-spectral-sequence` edge is being checked against the actual filtered relative Dolbeault double complex; the final manifest will list only results that its proof actually uses and this plan-level dependency discrepancy will remain recorded for the owner.
+
+The owner requires batch 68 to use `thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-projective-dolbeault-scalar-cohomology` from batch 67. The carrier has now appeared at `items/thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-projective-dolbeault-scalar-cohomology.md`. Its actual statement assumes full AC, covers finite-rank holomorphic vector bundles on `P^r(C)` for `r=0,1,2,3`, identifies analytic sheaf cohomology with the global smooth Dolbeault complex for every `q>=0`, and states the scalar structure-sheaf groups. Its complete proof and the supporting `lem-qftx-gaga-projective-cover-laurent-cohomology` proof were read; the exact H1/CP3 use in the batch-68 edge matches. `loadStep1Record`/`step1Decision` reports `Readiness record missing` for both batch-67 items, which is the unresolved prerequisite; draft status, precheck pending and absent independent approval are not used as blockers. The consuming proof is complete conditionally and batch-68 consumers remain `escalated` until the supplier readiness records are current and the interface is rechecked. The original shared dependency-level file recorded batch 68 at level 0 with no requirements; batch 68 now requires batch 67, and the shared file is left to its owner.
+
+## Source evidence
+
+Rawnsley, *On the Atiyah-Hitchin-Drinfeld-Manin vanishing theorem for cohomology groups of instanton bundles*, Math. Ann. 241 (1979), 43–56, full 14-page article read from the 15-page GDZ scan (front cover/terms plus pp. 43–56). Sections 1–3 and the full local calculation on pp. 47–56 supply the coupled fibre decomposition, the injective edge map, the smooth fibrewise correction, and the explicit `D^*D+R/6` coefficient. The line-by-line local derivation, including its convention translation, is reconstructed in local items rather than accepted from citation alone.
+
+Wells, *The Conformally Invariant Laplacian and the Instanton Vanishing Theorem*, in *Seminar on Differential Geometry*, Annals of Mathematics Studies 102 (1982), 483–498, full chapter read. Sections 2–3 support the double-fibration framework, the Penrose transform, the conformally invariant operator, and the Bochner argument. Wells cites earlier Penrose/Rawnsley/Hitchin work for some steps; the local proof supplies those steps rather than treating the chapter as a complete proof.
+
+Donaldson, *The ADHM construction of Yang–Mills instantons*, arXiv:2205.08639, complete 15-page paper read, §§3–6 (printed pp. 4–11). It supports the quaternionic real-line/Ward conventions and records the contour-transform context. Its printed p. 7 `R/8` coefficient is inconsistent with Rawnsley’s explicit `R/6` identity under the stated `R=12` unit-sphere convention; that coefficient is excluded from the proof and marked as a source discrepancy, not silently harmonized.
+
+The Rawnsley PDF was retrieved and visually inspected page by page; Wells’s complete 16-page book chapter and Donaldson’s complete arXiv PDF were read. `node tools/physics-support/source-fetch-check.mjs --coverage research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-68-source-coverage.json --stamp` passed: 3/3 sources fetch-verified, with no retries or documented drops. Rawnsley’s source hash prefix `6bd9e506846fa7c5` matches the previously observed article scan. The exact bytes, page counts/text length, and timestamps are in the coverage record. No measurement or physical inference is involved.
+
+## Item plan and next actions
+
+### Completed carriers so far
+
